<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Proposed anatomical blueprint for \"a flying-fish with enlarged pectoral fins that glides above the water\": 13 organ systems, 67 organs, 198 tissues and 257 cell types that Uberon and the Cell Ontology record for Exocoetidae." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "The description \"a flying-fish with enlarged pectoral fins that glides above the water\" was resolved to Exocoetidae (NCBITaxon:76072), most specific of the taxon words found (subclass of the others). From that taxon the tool proposes a body > organ system > organ > tissue > cell hierarchy of existing ontology terms: 13 organ systems, 67 organs, 198 tissues and 257 cell types, distinct terms counted once each. A tissue or cell type that is part of several organs is shown under each, which is why the file lists 250 tissue and 343 cell placements. Structures read from the description and kept regardless of ranking: \"pectoral fins\" as pectoral fin. Words the lexicon does not know, so that nothing in the blueprint stands for them: enlarged, glides, water. Of the 68 organs, 63 carry a taxon basis stated by the ontology for this taxon or an ancestor, 2 are stated only for all animals, and 3 are merely not excluded. The per-parent limits cut a further 171 organs, 784 tissues and 1182 cell types, each listed in the cited file with the query that found it; no structure named in the description was cut. The genome sketch is not a genome and is not part of this proposal: the Cell Ontology records marker gene products for 6 of the 257 cell types and nothing for the other 251. What was assembled is an anatomy, not an organism. This is a proposal assembled from ontology statements: it is not an observation of any organism, and no specimen, image or measurement enters it." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "ontology-derived design proposal" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Taxon Exocoetidae (NCBITaxon:76072), verified live as the most specific of the taxon words found. Selection: organ systems are subclasses of \"anatomical system\" in Uberon's core subsets, most general kept; organs are parts of a system in the major_organ, organ_slim, efo_slim or vertebrate_core subsets, ranked major organs first then by the number of cell types the Cell Ontology records for them, at most 6 per system; at most 6 tissues and 8 cell types per organ. Taxon filter: a structure is dropped when Uberon or the Cell Ontology states never_in_taxon on the term, a superclass, or a whole it is part of, for a taxon containing this one, or states in_taxon for a taxon unrelated to this one; for non-vertebrates the vertebrate_core subset is dropped as well. Absence of a constraint is not presence, which is why every organ carries its taxon evidence. Retrieved from Ubergraph on 2026-10-31T15:42:53.199Z; the cache pins the bytes. Unknown stays unknown: nothing was filled in where the ontologies are silent." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasEvidence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish-queries> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Take a curated anatomical atlas for Exocoetidae that was not used here, and count two things: structures the atlas lists at organ level that this blueprint omits, and organs this blueprint proposes that the atlas does not record for the taxon. A blueprint whose \"stated\" organs disagree with the atlas would falsify the selection rule; disagreement confined to the \"not excluded\" organs would instead show the ontology's taxon constraints are too sparse, which is the limit already recorded here." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Does not establish: that an individual of Exocoetidae has these structures, that the list is complete, or that any part of it was measured. Uberon's taxon constraints are sparse, so organs marked \"not excluded\" may be absent in the organism; tissues and cell types are ontology part_of statements, not counts from a specimen. Nothing here is a genome, and no function is predicted. In particular, this proposal is not evidence about the transition the motivating sentence imagines, in either direction." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/references> "darwin-origin/inputs/pg1228.txt#line=5639,5642" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Motivated by analogy with a sentence in Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, chapter VI; Project Gutenberg eBook 1228, at darwin-origin/inputs/pg1228.txt#line=5639,5642: \"it is conceivable that flying-fish, which now glide far through the air, slightly rising and turning by the aid of their fluttering fins, might have been modified into perfectly winged animals.\" Motivation by analogy only. Darwin names a hypothetical transition from gliding fish to winged animal; this blueprint lists the anatomy that Uberon and the Cell Ontology record for the living family, at one point in time. It is not evidence for or against the transition, it does not assert that these structures are the ones that would change, and nothing here is equivalent to or derived from the sentence. The mention is already recorded in organisms/merged/taxa.json under key gbif:2968." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-blueprint-a-flying-fish> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/main/organism-design/examples/a-flying-fish.json> .
}
